      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-14404",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u = e^{2x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 2e^{2x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{d}u = e^{2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|{e}^{2x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2x}+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that $e^{2x} + 1 > 0$ for all $x.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009212/example-14404.json",
          "file_sha256": "437b89b1bcbc196a04509f95038f5636b29292fffc32347615b5f5ff4b792ba1"
